WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.710
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Ugh, I
have all these teammates

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.250
asking me to spit out
new projects for them.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.950
But each one requires
special tweaks,

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.310
and then I have to apply
the standard configuration.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:08.310
ADAM OCSVARI: That's it?

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.870
Just use the Cloud Deployment
Manager to create projects.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.828
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh,
that's the infrastructure

00:00:12.828 --> 00:00:14.460
and configuration
as code tool, right?

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.020
Isn't that kind
of hard to set up?

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.570
ADAM OCSVARI: No, not at all,
if you use the Cloud Foundation

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.900
Toolkit template, such
as the Project Factory.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:21.240
MARK MIRCHANDANI: All right.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.390
Can we see how it works?

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.258
ADAM OCSVARI: Sure thing.

00:00:24.258 --> 00:00:26.738
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.570
The Cloud Foundation Toolkit
has a lot of great templates

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:33.950
to get started from.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.880
Let's begin with some easy
firewall rules, as a warm up.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.730
Looking up a template
at the CFT repository,

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.340
you will find a readme,
how to use the template,

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.790
with a ready-to-go
example configuration.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:50.990
First, let's go in the repo and
jump into the templates folder.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:52.910
Now, copy the
example file and fill

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.610
out the mandatory properties
about your environment--

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.910
in this case, the VPC name,
which is the network where

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.550
you want the firewalls to live.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.390
For now, let's use
the default network.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.170
Make sure you enable the
required APIs, Compute

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.030
and Deployment
Manager, and then we

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.350
are ready to launch
the deployment.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.840
Using the GCloud
CLI, our resources

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.850
will be created via
Deployment Manager,

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.590
within a couple of seconds.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:21.590
This shows up in the
Cloud console immediately.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:24.980
If we go back to the config
and change some properties,

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.442
we can update the resources
during its lifecycle,

00:01:27.442 --> 00:01:28.400
via Deployment Manager.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.230
If we refresh the
list of firewalls,

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:37.460
we can see the changes.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.092
Finally, by deleting
the deployment,

00:01:39.092 --> 00:01:41.050
we clean up all the
resources which we created.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.070
Now comes the real deal.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.310
We create a shared
VPC host project,

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:53.930
adding subnetworks in it.

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:56.330
Then we create a guest
project and attaching it

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.310
to one of those subnets.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.410
First, let's set our
environment variables

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.890
and enable the required APIs,
based on prerequisite section

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.270
of the readme.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.240
Don't forget to allow Deployment
Manager to attach your billing

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.781
account to projects.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.410
After copying and
filling out the example,

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.050
open the configuration
file and edit at the end.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.735
Because, first, we create
the VPC host project.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.380
Now, let's launch
the deployment.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.970
It usually takes a
couple of minutes

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.967
to create all of
these resources.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.880
Now, we copy the network
template example and launch it

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.778
with all the default settings
in the VPC host project,

00:02:52.778 --> 00:02:53.695
which we just created.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.900
Last step is to copy the project
example configuration again,

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.250
for the guest project.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.530
Fill out the
environment properties,

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.710
and make sure we are referencing
the recently created subnet

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.680
we wish to attach
this project to.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.240
After the same
create comment, we

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.120
can see, at the Cloud
console, the two projects--

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.820
the network with the
subnets at the host project

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.190
and the attached guest project.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:23.970
There you go.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.370
You just implemented the
automated project factory

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.990
in your organization.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:30.660
Check out our GitHub repository,
the Deployment Manager

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.330
best practices, and some
further bedside stories,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:34.740
down at the description.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.157
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
And don't forget

00:03:36.157 --> 00:03:38.760
to subscribe for more great
Google Cloud Platform content.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

